        "Most of us were taught, from our first years at school, that mistakes are something to be avoided. A red mark on a test meant we had failed, and a page with no red marks meant we had succeeded. {u:(1):It is hardly surprising, then, that many adults feel a small wave of shame whenever they are shown to be wrong.} Yet a growing body of research suggests that this attitude may be getting things backwards.",
        "In one well-known type of experiment, students are asked to guess the answers to questions before they have studied the material. Naturally, most of their guesses are wrong. Afterwards, they are given the correct answers. When they are tested a few days later, these students often remember the material better than students who simply read the answers from the start. {u:(2):The act of guessing, even badly, seems to prepare the mind to hold on to the truth when it finally arrives.}",
        "Why should this be? One explanation is that a wrong guess creates a kind of tension. We have made a prediction, and the prediction has failed. {u:(3):That small surprise draws our attention to exactly the point we did not understand}, and attention ranks among the chief ingredients of memory. A student who reads the answer without guessing has nothing to be surprised by, so the information may pass through the mind without leaving much of a mark.",
        "There is, however, an important condition. Mistakes help us learn only when we find out that they are mistakes, and only when we feel safe enough to look at them closely. {b:(4)}. In a classroom where every wrong answer is laughed at, students quickly learn to stay silent rather than risk a guess. They protect themselves, but they lose the very experience that would have helped them most.",
        "Some engineers understand this well. In the aviation industry, pilots are encouraged to report their own errors, even small ones that no one else noticed, and they are generally not punished for doing so. The reports are collected and studied so that the same mistake does not happen twice, on any plane, anywhere. {u:(6):This system works because it treats an error not as a personal failure to be hidden but as information to be shared.}",
